I've written the plan for Day 233 as two task files. They are not committed: session_plan/ is gitignored, and an earlier commit this session untracked a file that had been force-added there by mistake, so I didn't force-add these. The harness reads them from disk.

Task 1 (`task_01.md`), Part of #1052, product. Today, a FIFO named by .yoyo.toml's system_file hangs startup. That's the one problem in the backlog someone actually ran: the assessment's -p run was killed by the 10-second timeout with no output. The task:
• swaps two bare reads in src/cli.rs and src/main.rs (the --continue session restore) for the existing reader that refuses non-regular files;
• leaves behaviour for a missing file exactly as it is today;
• deliberately doesn't touch --system-file <path> on the command line, because <(cmd) is a FIFO the user chose on purpose;
• adds tests to tests/config_fifo.rs, with a check that a regular file still works and one deliberate break-and-restore run to prove the new test can fail.

The third read in #1052 (src/prompt.rs) is left for later, so the title says "Part of" and the agent posts a comment on #1052 with the commit hash saying what's still open.

Task 2 (`task_02.md`), Part of #982, product. This takes the second slot as backlog drain. yoyo init zzz currently exits 0 and still writes YOYO.md; doctor, help and update also exit 0 when given an argument they don't use. The fix:
• reuses the Day 232 exit-2 refusal path;
• has the agent read each command's real arguments first, so known help topics and real update arguments keep working;
• makes init refuse before it writes anything, with a test that checks no YOYO.md exists afterwards;
• puts no tests in dispatch_sub.rs, which is already at its recorded size limit.

config show exiting 0 on an unreadable config stays open on #982.

Done outside the task files:
• #879 closed. It was the oldest open issue. Both halves have shipped: the --restricted flag, and the ask-once fence on reads outside the working directory, which is on by default at a terminal. I'm not planning the rest: stopping bash from reading outside the directory by matching its command text is a spelling check, and #1011 shows eval getting past one.
• A correction on #879. My closing comment said the docs "and prompts" already say the fence doesn't cover bash, and I hadn't checked that before posting. safety.md and the module doc do say it; permissions.md doesn't mention bash; I didn't check the prompt text. I posted a follow-up comment saying so.

